## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React re-renders a component when its props change. Change is determined by `===` (reference equality), not deep equality. Primitives compare by value, but objects and functions compare by identity.

```jsx
// Re-renders every time Parent renders — new object reference each render
<Child config={{ theme: "dark" }} />

// Re-renders every time Parent renders — new function reference each render
<Child onClick={() => doSomething()} />
```

Every render of the parent creates a new `{}` or `() => {}`, so the child always sees "new" props, even if the values are identical.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if all props are shallowly equal.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ onClick, config }) => <button onClick={onClick}>{config.label}</button>);
```

Useless without also stabilizing the props being passed in.

---

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.

```jsx
// Stable reference; only changes when `id` changes
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

Use it when passing callbacks to `React.memo`-wrapped components or as dependencies of other hooks.

---

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value (including objects).

```jsx
// Stable object reference; only changes when `theme` changes
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme, size: "lg" }), [theme]);
```

Use it for:
- Objects/arrays passed as props to memoized components
- Expensive computations
- Values used as hook dependencies

**Don't overuse these.** Memoization has overhead. Only reach for them when you've identified a real performance problem or are intentionally stabilizing references.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context value change re-renders **every consumer**, regardless of which part of the value they use.

```jsx
// Bad — new object every render, all consumers re-render constantly
function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      <Tree />
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Fix 1 — memoize the value:**
```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
```

**Fix 2 — split contexts.** Separate stable state (dispatch/setters) from frequently-changing state (data). Components that only dispatch never re-render on data changes.

```jsx
<UserDispatchContext.Provider value={setUser}>
  <UserDataContext.Provider value={user}>
    <Tree />
  </UserDataContext.Provider>
</UserDispatchContext.Provider>
```

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders on every keystroke in the parent's input.

```jsx
function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [filters, setFilters] = useState({ sort: "asc" });

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList
        filters={filters}              // new object reference every render
        onFilterChange={f => setFilters(f)} // new function every render
      />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ filters, onFilterChange }) {
  // renders on every keystroke even though filters didn't change
}
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `filters` actually changes.

```jsx
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ filters, onFilterChange }) {
  // only renders when filters or onFilterChange reference changes
});

function Dashboard() {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");
  const [filters, setFilters] = useState({ sort: "asc" });

  const stableFilters = useMemo(() => filters, [filters]);
  const handleFilterChange = useCallback(f => setFilters(f), []);

  return (
    <>
      <input value={query} onChange={e => setQuery(e.target.value)} />
      <ExpensiveList
        filters={stableFilters}
        onFilterChange={handleFilterChange}
      />
    </>
  );
}
```

Typing in the input updates `query`, but `stableFilters` and `handleFilterChange` references are stable, so `ExpensiveList` skips its render entirely.

---

**Decision rule:** profile first with React DevTools Profiler, then apply `memo` + `useCallback`/`useMemo` surgically at the boundaries where you see wasted renders.
